🚽 Test case: issue #389 review -- returning a local array through decay.
🚽
🚽 `bussin xs;` from a pointer-returning function decays to `&xs[0]`. When
🚽 xs is the function's own local, its storage is freed as the call
🚽 returns, so every use of the result reads freed memory -- under ASan, a
🚽 heap-use-after-free in the interpreter itself. C compilers diagnose it
🚽 (-Wreturn-local-addr); decay is what makes this spelling possible here,
🚽 so it is refused at the decay site, for the array itself, arithmetic on
🚽 it, and an array field of a local struct.
🚽
🚽 Still accepted, and checked by array_decay.brainrot / the passing
🚽 functions below: a `salty` (static) local, which outlives the call, and
🚽 an array reached through a pointer parameter, which is the caller's.

gang Box {
    rizz vals[3];
};

rizz *local_array() {
    rizz xs[3] = {1, 2, 3};
    bussin xs;
}

rizz *local_array_plus() {
    rizz xs[3] = {1, 2, 3};
    bussin xs + 1;
}

rizz *local_struct_field() {
    gang Box b;
    bussin b.vals;
}

rizz *static_array() {
    salty rizz keep[3] = {4, 5, 6};
    bussin keep;
}

rizz *through_pointer(gang Box *bp) {
    bussin bp.vals;
}

skibidi main {
    yapping("side effect");
    bussin 0;
}
